window.docContents[206].resolve({"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Algebraic-number-and-algebraic-integer-via-eigenvalues-of-matrices/#Chapter5___Definition5___2___2":{"contents":"Definition 5.2.2. z \\in \\mathbb{C} is an algebraic number, (respectively, an algebraic integer), if z is an eigenvalue of a matrix with rational (respectively, integer) entries.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.2. Algebraic numbers and algebraic integers\tAlgebraic number and algebraic integer via eigenvalues of matrices","header":"Algebraic number and algebraic integer via eigenvalues of matrices","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Algebraic-number-and-algebraic-integer-via-eigenvalues-of-matrices/#Chapter5___Definition5___2___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator/Complex___-real___-and-quaternionic-type-of-irreducible-representations/#Chapter5___Definition5___1___1":{"contents":"Definition 5.1.1. We say that V is\n\n* of complex type if V \\not\\cong V^*,* of real type if V has a nondegenerate symmetric form invariant under G,* of quaternionic type if V has a nondegenerate skew form invariant under G.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.1. Frobenius-Schur indicator\tComplex, real, and quaternionic type of irreducible representations","header":"Complex, real, and quaternionic type of irreducible representations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator/Complex___-real___-and-quaternionic-type-of-irreducible-representations/#Chapter5___Definition5___1___1"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Section-3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras-___-heading/#Chapter3___Introduction_to_3___5___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.5. Finite dimensional algebras\tSection 3.5: Finite dimensional algebras — heading","header":"3.5. Finite dimensional algebras","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Section-3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras-___-heading/#Chapter3___Introduction_to_3___5___heading-1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Partition___-Young-diagram___-Young-tableau___-row-and-column-subgroups/#Chapter5___Definition5___12___1":{"contents":"Definition 5.12.1. A partition \\lambda of n is a representation of n in the form n = \\lambda_1 + \\lambda_2 + \\cdots + \\lambda_p, where \\lambda_i are positive integers and \\lambda_i \\geq \\lambda_{i+1}.\nTo such \\lambda we will attach a Young diagram Y_\\lambda, which is the union of rectangles -j \\leq y \\leq -j + 1, 0 \\leq x \\leq \\lambda_j in the coordinate plane, for j = 1, \\ldots, p. Clearly, Y_\\lambda is the union of n unit squares (i, j) := \\{(x, y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 \\mid -j \\leq x \\leq -j + 1, i - 1 \\leq x \\leq i\\}, j = 1, \\ldots, p, i = 1, \\ldots, \\lambda_j. A Young tableau corresponding to Y_\\lambda is the result of filling the numbers 1, \\ldots, n into the squares of Y_\\lambda in some way (without repetitions). For example, we will consider the Young tableau T_\\lambda obtained by filling in the numbers in increasing order, left to right, top to bottom.\n\nWe can define two subgroups of S_n corresponding to T_\\lambda:\n\n1. The row subgroup P_\\lambda: the subgroup which maps every element of \\{1, \\ldots, n\\} into an element standing in the same row in T_\\lambda.2. The column subgroup Q_\\lambda: the subgroup which maps every element of \\{1, \\ldots, n\\} into an element standing in the same column in T_\\lambda.\n\nClearly, P_\\lambda \\cap Q_\\lambda = \\{1\\}.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.12. Representations of $S_n$\tPartition, Young diagram, Young tableau, row and column subgroups","header":"Partition, Young diagram, Young tableau, row and column subgroups","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Partition___-Young-diagram___-Young-tableau___-row-and-column-subgroups/#Chapter5___Definition5___12___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/#chapter-05___section-5-8":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results","header":"5.8. Induced representations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/#chapter-05___section-5-8"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Section-9___4___-Homological-dimension/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___4___heading-1":{"contents":"Let A be a ring, and let M be a left A-module.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\t9.4. Homological dimension\tSection 9.4: Homological dimension","header":"9.4. Homological dimension","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___4___-Homological-dimension/Section-9___4___-Homological-dimension/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___4___heading-1"}});
